# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: The Drone Does Not Know the Bus Has Turned

**Story & Context:** At 82 BPM, Lira opens alone with a D drone and a three-note melody-cell above it. Tomas enters with muted kick, brushed ghost snare, and one dusty backbeat. Paolo first treats D as tonic, then shifts to G beneath the unchanged drone so the same pitch becomes the fifth. June withholds the third from her Rhodes voicing, creating a Drone Blindspot for two bars before revealing a suspended major-9 color. In the second verse, Paolo moves through C and A roots beneath the D drone, making it function first as ninth and then suspended fourth. Nothing changes in Lira's drone string. The emotional movement comes entirely from the changing floor. The chorus adds contrabass clarinet only after Paolo drops out, allowing one low acoustic note to redefine the drone yet again.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Rain-streaked provincial bus window at night, route lights bending in the glass as the vehicle turns while one fixed red radio-tower light remains centered in the distance, dark blue and oxidized green palette. Original sparse lyric fragments: “the bus already turned / the note stayed where it was / same rain on a different road / I looked up too late.”

## Track 2: Root Missing from the Weather Report

**Story & Context:** At 88 BPM, this is the EP's most rhythmically active track. Tomas uses a chopped original break with dusty snare, low kick, brushed sixteenth ghosts, and abrupt half-bar gaps. Lira keeps the drone constant while playing only short melody-string replies. June uses rootless minor-9 and dominant-9 Rhodes shells, while Paolo repeatedly enters late with the actual root after the listener has already formed another interpretation. Each delayed bass entrance functions as a reveal. The middle section uses Breakbeat Eclipse: drums collapse to brush noise and rim while bass walks through a chromatic Shadow Bass Turn underneath the drone. Tape Horizon consists of a barely audible loop of rain hitting a metal awning and one low electric-piano tone. The loop never changes even when the chord sequence does.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Old weather bulletin typed on cream paper with the word “ROOT” absent from one line, rain marks bleeding the carbon copy, one bus-route map folded underneath, red broadcast pencil on top. Original sparse lyric fragments: “root missing / forecast unchanged / the storm has another address / read the line again.”

## Track 3: Monsoon Room with One String Left On

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 72 BPM and begins with a Still String Bridge rather than a conventional intro. Lira sounds only the drone string while June plays widely spaced Rhodes sus2 and minor-9 voicings without bass. Mara introduces a soft Tape Horizon loop made from room hum and reversed Rhodes decay. After sixteen bars, Paolo enters on a root that makes the drone sound unexpectedly bright, then moves downward by semitone into a darker interpretation. Lira's melody string finally appears almost two minutes into the track. Tomas adds brushes, muted floor tom, and one kick every other bar. In the final minute, melody, drums, Rhodes, and bass disappear sequentially. The fixed drone remains alone with the tape loop. Mara stops the tape before Lira stops the string, so the EP ends with the acoustic reference point exposed and unaccompanied.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty broadcasting room during monsoon blackout, one battery-powered green desk lamp, kudyapi resting beside a reel-to-reel deck, a single string visibly vibrating while rain runs down dark windows. Original sparse lyric fragments: “one string left on / power gone / the room moved without me / let the rain choose the chord.”

